During my tenure as a Software Developer Intern at ZeonAi Labs,
SQL played a central role in designing scalable systems and ensuring data reliability across multiple high-impact projects.
My strongest application of SQL was during the development of the Indian Airforce Document Management System (DMS),
where I worked extensively with MySQL to build a secure, efficient, and multi-tenant data architecture.

One of my key responsibilities was optimizing the multi-tenant relational schema, 
where I restructured tables, indexes,
and query patterns to support SaaS-scale consumption. 
This included designing normalized and partition-aware tables to isolate tenant data while maintaining fast query performance.
As system load grew, certain SQL queries became bottlenecks. To address this,
I implemented a Redis-based queuing and caching layer, 
offloading repetitive read operations while ensuring consistency with the MySQL backend.
This approach reduced database load and improved throughput by 35%.

I also engineered concurrent metadata persistence for a Google Drive–style file/folder upload module.
Here, I designed SQL transactions that maintained referential integrity even under high-volume parallel writes. 
This improved upload efficiency by 40% while ensuring that metadata updates remained atomic.

Security also required the application of SQL. While building the RBAC-based multi-tenant permission system,
I wrote SQL queries and joins that enforced user-role mappings, hierarchical access rules,

and tenant-level scoping. This ensured that sensitive data remained isolated and audit-friendly.

Across both major projects, I consistently used SQL for complex data transformations,
ensuring ACID compliance, preventing race conditions, and maintaining data correctness under high concurrency.
Overall, SQL was instrumental in enabling scalability, robustness, and performance for mission-critical systems I contributed to.
